CHAPTERS = [
('Introduction', [
('Version control and team context', 'Software work changes continuously: requirements evolve, defects are corrected, and teams contribute in parallel. Version control records these changes as an ordered history so that a developer can compare revisions, recover earlier states, and coordinate work across branches. Git makes this history distributed; each clone can contain the complete object database and local branch references. Hosted services add identity, review, issue tracking, and automation around the repository. These facilities solve source history and collaboration problems, but they do not automatically make every repository change subject to one consistent governance policy.', 'HAKI is designed as a governance workspace around repositories rather than as a new source-control engine. The project explores how a team can associate a proposed change with a repository, branch, base revision, and exact head commit, then attach automated validation and human review evidence to that immutable revision. The distinction matters: Git remains responsible for version history, while HAKI supplies an additional coordination layer.'),
('Governance gap', 'A change can be technically valid and still be difficult to assess. The reviewer may not know which revision was scanned, whether a policy check ran on the same commit that was approved, or whether a later push invalidated the approval. Results may be spread across CI logs, comments, local tools, and chat. Small teams often compensate with manual checklists, but those checklists can be skipped or applied inconsistently. The central problem is therefore evidence continuity: the information needed to decide whether a change may proceed is not always bound together.', 'The design response in HAKI is to represent repository governance as a lifecycle. The important objects are a submission, validation run, finding, review decision, and optional pull request. A commit SHA is the join point between them. The database schema stores base and head SHA on a submission and reviewed SHA on a review, making it possible to reason about whether the human decision refers to the revision under discussion.'),
('Project goals and boundaries', 'This project aims to build a usable workspace for repository organization and to establish backend foundations for controlled change review. The goals include consistent identity handling, team and role representation, GitHub installation metadata, submission tracking, asynchronous scanner execution, normalized findings, and traceable review decisions. A successful academic prototype should demonstrate a coherent architecture and measurable implementation evidence. It should also state what is not finished so that a proposed feature is not mistaken for a deployed production capability.', 'The current frontend provides the public HAKI landing page, authentication screens, repository dashboard and workspace, file-tree navigation, settings, import, and About pages. The local client store handles repository and file demonstration flows in browser storage. The server contains a separately implemented API foundation for authentication, GitHub OAuth, submission validation jobs, reviews, and database models. The CLI source is currently only a scaffold. These layers are described separately throughout this report.'),
]),
('Purpose, Scope and Objectives', [
('Purpose', 'The purpose of HAKI is to make repository work easier to inspect and govern. A team should be able to see what repository a submission belongs to, what exact change was evaluated, which checks produced findings, who made the review decision, and whether an external pull request was created. The intended benefit is not simply another dashboard; it is a more structured chain from source revision to decision. The platform should preserve established GitHub workflows while adding a consistent record of policy and accountability.', 'The project is built as a full-stack web application with a React client, an Express API, shared validation schemas, Prisma persistence, and worker-oriented validation jobs. The deliverable for the present implementation is a working local workspace UI and a backend foundation whose modules and tests can be inspected. External GitHub operations and complete end-to-end workflows depend on configured credentials and runtime services, so they are identified as integration work rather than assumed to be active.'),
('Functional scope', 'The user-facing scope covers public information about HAKI, account entry, repository listing and creation, importing, activity summaries, repository file browsing, code display, and configuration screens. The backend scope includes registration and login routes, refresh-token rotation, logout, GitHub OAuth routes, access-token middleware, submission validation endpoints, and review-related routes. The validation worker retrieves a fixed commit archive, creates a temporary workspace, invokes Gitleaks, Semgrep, and Trivy, normalizes results, stores findings, updates run status, and cleans up files.', 'In the data model, roles include OWNER, ADMIN, DEVELOPER, and REVIEWER. Submission statuses include DRAFT, SUBMITTED, VALIDATING, IN_REVIEW, APPROVED, CHANGES_REQUESTED, REJECTED, BLOCKED, PUBLISHING, PUBLISHED, and CANCELLED. These values establish a domain vocabulary, but the existence of an enum does not prove every transition is exposed in the current UI. The report differentiates schema support from completed user flows.'),
('Out-of-scope and objectives', 'HAKI does not replace Git, provide a new merge algorithm, or guarantee that code is secure merely because scanners run. It does not eliminate the need for review, branch protection, tested recovery procedures, or correct secret handling. Multi-provider support, mature policy configuration, notification delivery, rich analytics, production operations, and an implemented CLI are future work unless separately verified. Establishing these boundaries keeps the project manageable and makes its results credible.', 'The measurable objectives are to provide a clear repository workspace, represent teams and repository identity in a relational schema, reject invalid or unauthorized API requests, keep long-running validation work out of request handlers, normalize findings across tools, and bind review evidence to an exact revision. Each objective can be evaluated through source inspection, automated tests, a local UI walkthrough, and integration tests against real GitHub and infrastructure credentials.'),
]),
('Problem Statement and Motivation', [
('Inconsistent change controls', 'Teams often adopt code review and automated checks gradually. One repository may enforce required reviews while another accepts direct pushes; one CI workflow may run secret scanning while another does not. These differences can be justified, but when they are undocumented they create uncertainty. Reviewers cannot easily tell whether a check is absent intentionally or was forgotten. A centralized governance view can clarify which controls are expected and can make a missing result visible before a decision is recorded.', 'HAKI models team settings and repository settings as JSON configuration attached to their owner entities. This offers flexibility for policy evolution, while also creating a validation responsibility: JSON configuration must have a schema, version, defaults, and migration plan. Without these controls, a flexible JSON field can become an undocumented collection of flags. The current schema provides the persistence point; a hardened policy editor and compatibility layer remain engineering tasks.'),
('Evidence fragmentation and stale decisions', 'A code change can move after an approval. If a reviewer approves branch name “feature-x” and that branch advances, the name alone does not prove which bytes were approved. The same issue applies to scanner evidence: a “passed” badge is meaningful only if it refers to the evaluated revision and a defined policy version. When evidence is fragmented, audit reconstruction becomes slow and incomplete. This is especially important when a security finding is later associated with a release.', 'The HAKI data model includes the submitted headCommitSha and a reviewedCommitSha for every review. The validation run is associated with the submission and stores a commit SHA, while findings are associated with that validation run. This structure supports an integrity rule: a current approval should only satisfy a gate when its reviewed SHA matches the submission SHA and all required validation evidence is complete for that same SHA.'),
('Operational visibility', 'A workflow that reports only “failed” does not help a developer recover. Failure can arise from invalid input, denied repository permissions, an unavailable GitHub archive, a scanner execution error, a queue outage, or a policy violation. These cases need different responses. A scanner finding is a completed evaluation with an adverse result; a scanner crash is an infrastructure failure and should not be reported as a clean scan. Operational visibility therefore depends on distinguishing state, reason, and remediation.', 'The worker marks a validation run RUNNING, records completion or an error code, and uses a transaction to persist findings and update run/submission status. On errors it records a stable processor failure code and rethrows so BullMQ can apply configured retry behavior. Error detail is intentionally not returned from the shown worker catch block, reducing accidental leakage but also limiting diagnostics. Structured safe logging and observability fields are a next step.'),
]),
('Literature Review and Related Systems', [
('Version control foundations', 'Git uses content-addressed objects and references to represent repository history. A commit identifies a tree, parent commit or commits, author and committer metadata, and a message. Branches are movable references, while commit identifiers refer to specific objects. This distinction is relevant to governance: policies should evaluate immutable object IDs rather than assuming a branch label will remain fixed. Git also supports local history and branching, enabling developers to work independently before sharing changes through a host.', 'HAKI adopts this model by capturing source and target branch names for human readability while retaining base and head commit SHAs for integrity. A branch name helps a person locate work; a SHA is used to identify the specific tree considered by validation or review. If a submission is updated, its evidence must be recomputed or explicitly treated as stale.'),
('Hosted collaboration and review', 'Platforms such as GitHub provide remote repositories, pull requests, code review, access control, and automation. A pull request is a useful human interface for reviewing a proposed merge, but it is not itself proof that a particular policy was followed. Required checks and branch protection can improve consistency. Integrations must request only the permissions needed for their behavior, handle rate limits, and maintain a secure relationship between installation identity and repository access.', 'The project includes a GitHub App installation model, installation-token helper, OAuth controller, and archive download path used for validation. GitHub App permissions should be selected according to actual endpoints and webhook subscriptions. The project should document that permission set and use test installations before production. The current API foundation targets GitHub rather than multiple hosting providers.'),
('Automated review and security scanning', 'Static analysis, secret detection, and dependency scanning identify different classes of risk. Secret scanners look for credential-like patterns and may produce false positives. SAST tools inspect source code against rules; dependency scanners compare packages or images against vulnerability data. A result must include tool, rule or package, severity, location, and a stable identity where feasible. Tool versions and rules change over time, so a report should preserve enough metadata to explain why a finding appeared.', 'HAKI’s worker invokes Gitleaks, Semgrep, and Trivy sequentially against a source archive for a specific commit. Each tool has a parser that maps native output into a normalized finding shape. Scanner exit codes are interpreted as either completed-with/without findings or execution failure. The architecture improves aggregation, while reproducibility still requires recording exact scanner versions, rule configuration revision, and artifact digest.'),
]),
('Existing and Proposed System', [
('Existing workflow', 'In a conventional workflow, a developer creates a branch, pushes commits, opens a pull request, and relies on CI and reviewers to decide whether the change can merge. This workflow is flexible and widely understood. Its quality depends on repository configuration, team habits, and integration coverage. Each repository can have a different set of actions, reviewer rules, and security tools. Evidence may be visible, but the team may need to visit multiple interfaces to assemble it.', 'HAKI is proposed as an additional governance view that connects a submission record, commit-specific validation, review decisions, and an optional pull request. This central record can support consistent status reporting across repositories. It should complement GitHub’s controls and never quietly bypass them. If a merge gate cannot be confirmed, a safe design blocks progression and explains the missing evidence.'),
('Proposed lifecycle', 'The design lifecycle begins with repository authorization and a submission that records its source branch, target branch, base SHA, and head SHA. The API accepts a validation request and enqueues work rather than running scanners inside the request process. A worker retrieves a fixed revision, executes scanners, converts reports into findings, and records a final status. A reviewer then makes a decision against the revision. A later push changes the revision and invalidates the prior decision for gate purposes.', 'The implemented worker currently transitions validation status from QUEUED to RUNNING to COMPLETED or FAILED. On completion, findings and status updates are written in a transaction. Submission state is moved from VALIDATING to SUBMITTED after worker completion; this is a narrower implementation than a full state machine containing every enum value. Full transition orchestration, approval checks, and publish decisions require additional integration.'),
('Comparison and adoption', 'The proposed system centralizes governance context without asking every developer to abandon their existing Git commands. It offers a browser workspace and has a server API foundation for validation and review. Adoption should be incremental: begin with read-only repository metadata, add validation, then add review decisions and publishing only after identity, permissions, and operational controls are reliable. Such sequencing reduces risk and helps teams understand what evidence is collected.', 'A pilot should compare time to understand a change, completeness of validation evidence, number of stale approvals detected, and frequency of blocked transitions. These measures should be collected with consent and without treating raw usage counts as quality. The current local frontend repository view is a prototype workflow; real multi-user behavior needs server-backed repository ownership and integration tests.'),
]),
('Project Category and Technology Stack', [
('Application type and architecture', 'HAKI is a web-based software engineering project with a React user interface, an Express API, shared schemas, relational persistence, asynchronous background jobs, and a command-line package scaffold. The codebase is organized as a monorepo with workspaces for client, server, CLI, and shared packages. This arrangement makes shared request contracts possible while keeping browser rendering, server-side authorization, and worker execution in separate runtime environments.', 'The frontend is JavaScript/JSX, React 19, Vite, React Router, Tailwind CSS integration, and Lucide icons. The backend is TypeScript using Express 5. PostgreSQL is accessed through Prisma with the PostgreSQL adapter. Redis and BullMQ support queue processing. Docker Compose configuration is located in infrastructure. These are source-verified stack claims; the report does not claim a production deployment.'),
('Why these choices fit', 'React provides component composition for pages such as dashboard, repository workspace, authentication, import, settings, and About. Express provides a direct route and middleware model. TypeScript on the server makes domain and API data structures more explicit. Prisma schema definitions provide a central view of entities, relations, indexes, and enums. Redis/BullMQ separate asynchronous work from short HTTP requests and provide job retry controls.', 'The stack is not inherently secure or scalable by default. Security depends on configuration, boundaries, and code. A TypeScript type is erased at runtime and cannot validate untrusted JSON, which is why shared Zod validation is used. Queue jobs can be duplicated, delayed, or retried; worker operations therefore need idempotency. Database migrations must be applied consistently before service startup.'),
('Workspace structure and scripts', 'The root package declares workspaces for the client, server, CLI, and shared contracts/schemas. The client package offers Vite development and production build scripts, plus linting. The server package offers a Vitest test command, TypeScript build, dev server, worker command, and queue test utility. The CLI package contains TypeScript source but its current entry module is an empty export, so a working command-line interface should not be advertised as complete.', 'This monorepo supports local development, but the root package does not provide a complete orchestration script. Developers must understand which service and infrastructure dependencies are required for a particular feature. An improvement is to document a verified setup matrix and provide root-level commands for starting the UI, API, database, Redis, and worker with clear health checks.'),
]),
('Requirement Analysis', [
('Stakeholders and actors', 'The system has several roles with different responsibilities. A developer creates and updates submissions. A reviewer evaluates evidence and records a decision. An administrator configures team membership and repository policy. An owner has responsibility for team-level administration. The system operator configures infrastructure and secrets but should not automatically receive broad application permissions. These responsibilities should be modeled explicitly so that the user interface and API enforce the same rules.', 'The Prisma schema defines OWNER, ADMIN, DEVELOPER, and REVIEWER team roles and ACTIVE or SUSPENDED membership status. It also defines GitHub user or organization installation types. The schema provides the vocabulary, while effective authorization requires checks that combine user identity, team membership, resource ownership, and requested action. A hidden or disabled UI control is not a sufficient access-control boundary.'),
('Functional and quality requirements', 'Functional requirements describe what the system should do: authenticate users, create teams, connect repositories, register a submission, run checks, record findings, support comments and decisions, and maintain an audit trail. Quality requirements describe how well it should behave: responsive interface, predictable API errors, secure token handling, bounded scanner execution, useful logs, recovery from retries, and accessible navigation. Each requirement should include a testable acceptance condition.', 'A practical acceptance condition for validation is: given a submission with a stored head SHA and an authorized GitHub installation, when the validation endpoint is called, the API returns an accepted/queued response without running tools in the request path; the worker processes that commit; and the client can later retrieve the final status and findings. This condition distinguishes an asynchronous job lifecycle from a synchronous scanner call.'),
('Prioritization and traceability', 'Requirements should be prioritized by user value and risk. Authentication, resource authorization, repository identity, and commit provenance are foundational because later features depend on them. Validation and evidence persistence follow. Review decisions and controlled publishing need both technical workflows and strong integrity checks. Rich analytics, provider expansion, and advanced settings are valuable but can follow once the core path is correct.', 'A traceability matrix should link each requirement to a module, a data entity, an acceptance test, and an implementation status. Status labels should be precise: implemented and verified, implemented but not end-to-end verified, designed, or future. This report uses that distinction in the implementation chapter and appendices so that reviewers can map claims to source files and test suites.'),
]),
('Feasibility and Project Planning', [
('Technical feasibility', 'The project uses widely adopted technologies and divides major responsibilities across browser, API, database, queue, and worker components. The stack is technically feasible for an academic prototype and can be run locally with the appropriate environment variables and services. The main complexity lies in integrating external GitHub access, securing untrusted archive processing, maintaining consistent submission state, and testing failure behavior across asynchronous boundaries.', 'A phased plan is appropriate. First stabilize authentication and persistence. Next connect GitHub installations and repository metadata. Then create submissions with immutable commit references. Add the validation worker and normalized findings, followed by review decisions bound to commit SHA. Only after those pieces are verified should the system create or update pull requests. The current source demonstrates parts of these phases, not a single finished pipeline.'),
('Operational and economic feasibility', 'An academic team can develop and test the application with commodity developer hardware. The prototype’s cost is mainly development time and the optional infrastructure required for PostgreSQL, Redis, containers, and hosted GitHub integration. A production service would add costs for durable storage, monitoring, backups, networking, scanner compute, and incident response. The cost model should include operational labor and support, not just cloud instance price.', 'For a classroom or local demonstration, services can run on one machine with modest resource limits. For production, scanner workspaces should be isolated from the API, concurrency should be bounded, temporary artifacts should be deleted, and metrics should capture queue age and failure rates. Economic feasibility improves when teams can adopt HAKI without migrating repositories or rewriting Git workflows.'),
('Schedule, risk, and governance', 'A project schedule should reserve time for integration and verification rather than assigning all effort to feature coding. External OAuth configuration, database migrations, container availability, and scanner behavior can delay the happy path. Legal and ethical review includes treatment of source code, repository metadata, secrets detected by scanners, and user activity records. The service should collect only the data needed to provide governance and should set an explicit retention policy.', 'The highest risks are unauthorized repository access, exposed credentials, archive extraction abuse, scanner resource exhaustion, stale approval, and false confidence from missing checks. Mitigations include narrow GitHub App permissions, signed or verified integration inputs, input validation, job timeouts, bounded retries, workspace cleanup, safe logs, and fail-closed transitions. Risk ownership should be assigned before a production pilot.'),
]),
('Hardware and Software Requirements', [
('Developer environment', 'A development machine needs a supported operating system, Node.js runtime, package manager, Git, and a modern browser. The frontend can be developed independently with Vite. Backend work also requires PostgreSQL and environment variables. Validation tasks add Redis, BullMQ worker processes, Docker, and the scanner executables or container images. Hardware requirements depend on the number of concurrent scans and the size of repositories.', 'A baseline development environment should have at least a multi-core CPU, 8 GB of memory, and several gigabytes of free storage for dependencies and temporary archives. These are planning estimates, not benchmark results from the current project. A test environment should be sized with representative repositories and resource caps, because scanner workloads can be much more demanding than the dashboard.'),
('Runtime dependencies and configuration', 'The client package depends on React, React DOM, React Router, Lucide icons, Vite, and Tailwind’s Vite plugin. The server uses Express, Prisma, PostgreSQL driver/adapter, Argon2, jose, BullMQ, ioredis, cookie-parser, CORS, dotenv, and validation packages. Shared packages contain contracts and schemas. The actual `package.json` files should be treated as the authoritative runtime inventory.', 'Environment configuration must not be committed with secrets. The server requires `FRONTEND_ORIGIN` at startup and has PostgreSQL and GitHub settings in its environment examples or infrastructure configuration. Production configuration should define TLS termination, trusted proxy behavior, cookie security flags, JWT secrets, GitHub App private key, database pool settings, Redis connection parameters, and scanner image versions.'),
('Compatibility, capacity, and support', 'The application should behave consistently on current Chromium, Firefox, and Safari releases, while responsive breakpoints support laptop and smaller screens. The user interface currently uses responsive utility classes; formal accessibility and cross-browser verification should be planned. Capacity is determined by API request volume, database connection capacity, Redis throughput, scanner CPU/memory, and artifact storage—not by the React client alone.', 'Operational runbooks should describe service health checks, database migration, queue inspection, worker restart, scanner image update, temporary workspace cleanup, credential rotation, and backup restoration. The API exposes a health endpoint returning service status and timestamp. A production health check should also verify the dependencies needed for critical paths, distinguishing process liveness from readiness.'),
]),
('System Analysis and Workflows', [
('Actors and primary flow', 'A developer selects a repository and starts a change. The system needs enough context to identify the base revision, head revision, source branch, and target branch. The submission moves into a state that permits validation. A worker executes checks, then the reviewer evaluates the recorded evidence and makes a decision. If the branch advances, the system must mark the existing evidence or approval as no longer current. A publishing step, if enabled, uses the authorized GitHub application.', 'The application’s current frontend workflow is primarily workspace-oriented: users authenticate, see repositories, create or import repository records, open a repository, browse folders, select files, view source text, and inspect README content below the selected code. The backend domain workflow is more advanced in its schema and validation modules. The report treats these as two connected but currently distinct workflows.'),
('State and transition analysis', 'A state machine makes legal progress explicit. For example, a submission may move from DRAFT to SUBMITTED, VALIDATING, IN_REVIEW, APPROVED, and PUBLISHED. A scanner failure should create a failed validation run, not a clean result. A change request should return work to the developer. A new commit should invalidate any previous commit-bound approval. Each transition should specify an authorized actor, required evidence, database changes, and whether it can be retried.', 'The schema has enumerated submission, validation, review, pull request, and finding status types. The worker currently handles QUEUED, RUNNING, COMPLETED, FAILED, and CANCELLED validation states at model level and returns the submission from VALIDATING to SUBMITTED after completion or final failure. This should be refined into explicit transition services with concurrency control and idempotency.'),
('Failure and recovery paths', 'Workflow analysis should include missing permissions, malformed SHAs, unavailable archives, corrupt ZIP files, scanner timeout, resource exhaustion, duplicate jobs, database transaction failure, queue interruption, reviewer role mismatch, and GitHub rate limiting. A failure path must preserve evidence needed to diagnose the issue without leaking source code or secrets. The user should see an actionable status while retry behavior remains bounded.', 'The worker resolves a validation workspace, writes an archive path adjacent to it, requests a GitHub installation token, downloads the archive at the run’s commit SHA, extracts it, invokes scanners, and cleans up in a `finally` block. Cleanup is best-effort. Future hardening should use unique directories, strict archive path validation, filesystem permissions, disk quotas, and safe retention for failed diagnostics.'),
]),
('System Architecture', [
('Logical layers', 'The architecture separates presentation, API, domain services, persistence, queue, worker, and external provider integration. The browser renders React components and sends user actions to application services. Express routes apply middleware and call controllers. Domain services enforce workflow rules. Prisma maps relational data. Redis/BullMQ coordinates asynchronous work. A dedicated worker processes an immutable source snapshot and writes results back to PostgreSQL.', 'This separation keeps long-running scanner execution out of API response latency and reduces the chance that a scanner crash terminates a web request process. It also introduces distributed-system concerns: queue messages and database state can temporarily disagree, jobs can be retried, and workers can stop mid-run. The architecture therefore needs idempotency keys, job reconciliation, and observable status transitions.'),
('Integration and trust boundaries', 'The trust boundaries include browser-to-API, API-to-database, API-to-queue, worker-to-GitHub, and worker-to-untrusted source archive. Each boundary needs authentication or authorization, input validation, constrained output, and failure handling. GitHub installation credentials are privileged secrets; user-uploaded repository content is untrusted. A worker must not execute arbitrary build scripts simply because it is scanning a repository.', 'The current validation worker extracts source and runs scanner commands, not project build commands. This is a safer baseline, but archive processing remains an attack surface. Containers should run as non-root, with restricted capabilities, read-only base filesystems where practical, no Docker socket, bounded CPU/memory, network egress controls, and short-lived workspaces. These protections must be verified in deployment configuration.'),
('Deployment topology and rationale', 'For development, the client, API, PostgreSQL, Redis, and worker may run locally or through Docker Compose. For a production topology, the API and worker can scale independently. The database should use managed or otherwise durable storage with backups. Redis should be operated with persistence and availability appropriate to queue guarantees. Source artifacts should remain temporary unless an explicit retention requirement is approved.', 'The repository contains a Docker Compose file under infrastructure and separate server commands for API development, tests, build, and validation worker. A complete deployment guide should specify network exposure, secrets injection, migration order, health probes, image pinning, and rollback. Scaling workers increases scanner throughput but also increases compute and external API pressure, so concurrency needs a deliberate limit.'),
]),
('Data Flow Diagrams and Use Cases', [
('Context and level-zero flow', 'At the system boundary, a developer or reviewer uses the browser client, while GitHub provides repository identity and commit archive data. The API authenticates users and validates the request. PostgreSQL stores users, teams, submissions, review records, and findings. Redis holds queued work. A worker fetches the archive and runs scanners. The result returns to the database and is later read by an authorized client.', 'A context-level data-flow diagram should make external actors and stores visible without pretending that every external flow is active in the present UI. HAKI currently exposes health, auth, user, submission, and review route groups. Repository and submission discovery may require further endpoints beyond the routes shown in the client-server integration. Diagram edges are therefore labeled as implemented, schema-supported, or planned.'),
('Validation sub-process', 'Validation begins when an authorized request references a submission. The controller asks the service to create or locate a run and enqueue a job. The worker loads the run and its submission, verifies repository installation status, creates a temporary directory, obtains an installation token, downloads a fixed commit archive, extracts it, executes Gitleaks, Semgrep, and Trivy, parses their reports, then stores normalized findings and final status transactionally.', 'Data-flow integrity depends on associating every output with validationRunId and commit SHA. A finding should preserve a tool name, severity, rule or identifier, message, file path, line range where available, and deterministic fingerprint. The report should also retain scanner version and configuration fingerprint in future schema revisions so that two runs can be compared meaningfully.'),
('Use cases and actor checks', 'A developer’s core use cases include sign in, inspect workspace, create a repository record, import repository metadata, browse the repository tree, open source content, and review README. Backend use cases include requesting validation and reading its status. Reviewers need to inspect findings and record a decision. Administrators manage roles and policies. An operator manages runtime dependencies and scanner images.', 'Use-case testing should verify both allowed and denied paths. For each operation, test a valid actor, unauthenticated request, wrong team, suspended membership, stale token, missing repository permission, and duplicate request. The UI should avoid exposing actions a user cannot take, while API tests confirm that access checks remain effective when a client calls an endpoint directly.'),
]),
('ER Model and Database Design', [
('Identity and teams', 'The `User` model stores a unique email, optional display name, optional password hash, verification and deletion timestamps, and timestamps. `OAuthAccount` connects a user to a provider identity and enforces uniqueness by provider account and by user/provider. `Team` has a unique slug. `TeamMember` connects users to teams with role and membership status. `TeamSettings` attaches JSON configuration to a team.', 'This model supports password and GitHub identity linkage while keeping provider identifiers separate from email. Cascade behavior on some identity relations removes dependent records with the user or team, while repository relations use restrictive deletion to protect provenance. Deletion policy must be reviewed against retention needs: hard deletion may conflict with audit traceability, so soft deletion and anonymization need an explicit policy.'),
('Repository and governance entities', '`GitHubInstallation` links a provider installation and account to a team. `Repository` stores provider ID, team and installation IDs, name, full name, owner login, default branch, URL, visibility, archive state, and timestamps. `RepositorySettings` contains repository policy JSON. A `Submission` records its title, description, branches, base SHA, head SHA, author, repository, status, and lifecycle timestamps.', 'The schema uses UUIDs for internal keys and BigInt for external GitHub numeric IDs. Unique constraints prevent duplicate provider repository IDs and duplicate full names within a team. Indexes support common lookups such as team membership by user/status, repository by team, submissions by repository/status/date, and recent audit events by team. Query patterns should be checked against production data volume.'),
('Review, validation, publishing, and audit', 'A `Review` records submission, reviewer, decision, reviewed commit SHA, body, and time. `ReviewComment` attaches line-oriented comments to a review. `ValidationRun` stores commit, status, timing, and error code, while `ValidationFinding` stores normalized output. `PullRequest` links a submission to an external pull request and its state. `AuditEvent` captures team, actor, action, entity, metadata, and time.', 'These relations make it possible to reconstruct a change lifecycle, but audit completeness depends on writing an event for each meaningful transition. JSON configuration and metadata need privacy review and schema versioning. Important invariant checks include that a review’s SHA matches the current submission at decision time and that only one current pull request is linked to a submission.'),
]),
('Module Description', [
('Frontend modules', 'The client is organized into page components and reusable interface elements. `App.jsx` defines routing and public/authenticated transitions. The dashboard loads repository and commit activity through the client service layer. `RepositoryWorkspace.jsx` renders a repository’s file tree, selected code, and README. Auth, About, settings, and import pages cover related entry points. Components such as the logo, workspace header, repository cards, modal, avatar, directory tree, and chatbot provide reuse.', 'The current UI is a local prototype for repository browsing and management. The service module stores users, repositories, files, commits, and sessions in browser local storage under a HAKI-specific key; auth calls use a local API origin. This mode is useful for presentation and local walkthroughs, but browser storage is not multi-user persistence or a secure production database. A server-backed repository API is needed for shared operation.'),
('Backend modules', 'The API app composes CORS, JSON parsing, cookies, route groups, and a health endpoint. Authentication controllers cover registration, login, refresh, logout, and GitHub OAuth. Middleware authenticates access tokens and applies request origin validation and rate limiting. Submission routes trigger and read validation; review routes support review operations. Prisma provides database access through a shared library.', 'The validation job modules handle queue construction, GitHub archive download, workspace creation and cleanup, archive extraction, scanner execution, finding parsing, and processor logic. Keeping parsers and scanners separated improves testability. Module boundaries should stay narrow: controllers handle HTTP shapes, services enforce business rules, and persistence changes remain transactional where state and findings must agree.'),
('CLI and integration boundary', 'A CLI can improve developer ergonomics by letting a user submit a revision or inspect validation status without switching to a browser. It should support a login flow, repository selection, clear confirmation before publish, and structured machine-readable output. A CLI also requires a secure local credential store or browser-mediated authentication and should avoid persisting long-lived secrets in plain text.', 'The repository’s current `cli/src/index.ts` contains only an empty module export, so this is not a functional CLI at the time of report generation. The package structure shows intended workspace placement, but commands, flags, tests, packaging, and release are still required. GitHub APIs and webhooks are additional integration boundaries that need signature verification and narrow permissions.'),
]),
('Process Logic and Algorithms', [
('Authentication and refresh rotation', 'The server hashes passwords with Argon2id and verifies them on login. Access tokens are issued and verified with the `jose` library. Refresh tokens are opaque random secrets stored in hashed form and associated with a family. On refresh, the server finds the token record, checks expiry and revocation, consumes the token, creates a successor, and returns a new cookie and access token. Reusing a consumed token is treated as evidence of token theft and can revoke the family.', 'The rotation service performs related database mutations inside a transaction and uses conditional updates to avoid consuming a token twice. Concurrent refresh attempts can race; the conditional update is important because only one should succeed. Tests cover valid rotation, expired tokens, reuse detection, and family revocation. Cookie flags and origin checks must be appropriate for the deployment’s HTTPS and frontend origin.'),
('Validation job algorithm', 'The API verifies the caller and request, then creates a validation run with a stable ID and enqueues a job. The worker updates the run to RUNNING, loads repository and installation records, rejects a suspended installation, makes a work directory, downloads a commit archive, extracts it, and runs the three scanners. Reports are parsed into a common finding representation. A transaction replaces prior findings for the run and marks the run complete.', 'The worker checks scanner exit codes and distinguishes expected “findings present” outcomes for Gitleaks and Semgrep from scanner process errors. Trivy requires a zero exit code in the current code. An error on the final attempt marks the run FAILED and returns the submission from VALIDATING to SUBMITTED, then rethrows. Backoff and maximum attempts are queue configuration concerns and should be aligned with failure classes.'),
('Authorization and integrity algorithms', 'Authorization should be evaluated from the authenticated user, active team membership, resource team, and action. A client-provided repository identifier is only a lookup key, not proof of permission. For GitHub operations, the application installation must be linked to the same team and must still be active. A review decision should store the commit SHA it concerns, and a publication gate should compare it to the current submission SHA.', 'A future decision function can be expressed as: allow publish only when the submission is current, required validations are complete for its head SHA, blocking findings are within policy, the required number and role of approvals exist for the same SHA, the repository is not archived, and the installation is active. The current schema supports parts of this rule; the full gate must be implemented and tested before use.'),
]),
('API Design and CLI', [
('API routing and contracts', 'The Express application exposes `/api/v1/health`, `/api/v1/auth`, `/api/v1/users`, and `/api/v1/submissions` route groups. API versioning provides a stable path for compatible changes. JSON requests should have documented schemas, bounded field lengths, stable error codes, and a response envelope. Shared schemas reduce drift between clients and server, but the server remains the authority for validation and authorization.', 'The health endpoint returns a success envelope with service name, status, and timestamp parsed through `HealthResponseSchema`. Auth routes include register, login, refresh, logout, and GitHub OAuth start/callback. The submissions group includes validation trigger and status retrieval protected by access-token middleware. Exact payload and response fields should be documented directly from controller and schema definitions; illustrative examples are marked accordingly.'),
('Security and error behavior', 'API errors should avoid exposing stack traces, credentials, scanner output containing secrets, or database internals. Validation errors should identify the invalid field without echoing sensitive values. Authentication failures should be uniform enough to avoid account enumeration. Rate limits should be applied to login and registration. Mutating requests using cookie credentials need origin or CSRF protections.', 'The app configures CORS for a single `FRONTEND_ORIGIN` and credentials, and auth routes apply request-origin validation and rate limiting. Authentication middleware protects validation routes. Future API hardening should add request IDs, structured error codes, consistent not-found behavior for inaccessible resources, pagination, idempotency keys for job creation, and OpenAPI documentation.'),
('CLI workflow and current status', 'A functional CLI would allow `haki auth login`, `haki submit`, `haki status`, and `haki findings` commands. It should detect a Git working tree, identify the head commit, validate the remote repository, and print the submission URL or status. It should support JSON output for CI and readable output for people. Secrets must be kept out of command history and logs.', 'The CLI is presently a scaffold only. The report therefore describes this workflow as a target rather than completed functionality. A safe implementation would prefer browser-based OAuth or short-lived tokens, store refresh credentials using operating-system facilities, support revocation, and never silently publish. CLI tests should include missing Git remote, detached HEAD, uncommitted changes, network failure, unauthorized repository, and expired credentials.'),
]),
('Security, Privacy and Governance', [
('Authentication controls', 'Password storage should use a deliberately slow password-hashing scheme; HAKI uses Argon2id. Access tokens are short-lived signed credentials and should be verified for issuer, audience, algorithm, expiry, and subject. Refresh tokens should be random, rotated, hashed at rest, scoped to a token family, and revocable. Cookies should use `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and appropriate `SameSite` behavior, with HTTPS required in production.', 'The code includes Argon2id password hashing and `jose` access-token signing/verification. The refresh-token model stores hashed token records, family identifiers, expiry, consumed time, and revocation data. The refresh service detects reuse. This is a substantial backend foundation; deployment settings such as strong secrets, TLS, secure cookies, backup, rotation, and monitoring remain essential and are environment-dependent.'),
('Request integrity and authorization', 'Credentialed browser requests need a narrow CORS policy and protection against cross-site request forgery. Origin validation should compare to configured allowed origins and fail safely when missing. Login endpoints need rate limiting and sensible lockout or abuse response. Authorization checks should be enforced server-side for each resource, not inferred from a client route or role label.', 'HAKI configures CORS to `FRONTEND_ORIGIN`, allows credentialed requests, and applies origin validation and rate limiters on registration, login, refresh, and logout paths. Access-token middleware protects submission validation routes. The exact cookie and proxy configuration must be checked in deployment. High-risk operations should emit audit events and include an explicit actor and entity association.'),
('Privacy, retention, and audit', 'Repository source can contain proprietary code and secrets. A validation service should minimize copied data, process a pinned archive, avoid storing entire source trees, redact sensitive scanner output, and delete temporary workspaces. Audit records should capture who did what and when without storing tokens or raw secrets. Retention periods should be proportionate to legal, operational, and academic needs.', 'The worker deletes the downloaded archive and removes the validation workspace in a best-effort cleanup path. AuditEvent schema stores team, optional actor, action, entity type/id, JSON metadata, and timestamp. Privacy review should define data classification, access logging, user deletion behavior, backup retention, scanner report retention, and support access. Safe logging tests should assert that passwords and tokens never enter logs.'),
]),
('Validation and Security Scanners', [
('Pipeline design', 'Security validation is modeled as asynchronous work because scanners can take longer than an HTTP request and have different resource requirements. The API receives a request, the producer enqueues a job, a worker retrieves the source snapshot, and results are written to the database. The queue can retry transient failures, while the run record exposes progress to users. Each job must be tied to an immutable commit.', 'The current processor updates status, loads the submission and repository installation, obtains an installation token, downloads an archive for the run’s commit, extracts it, invokes Gitleaks, Semgrep, and Trivy, parses findings, and writes the result. It does not execute a repository’s build or test scripts. This reduces execution risk and keeps scanner behavior more predictable.'),
('Tool roles and normalization', 'Gitleaks searches for potentially exposed credentials. Semgrep applies static analysis rules to source code. Trivy examines dependency and container artifacts for known vulnerabilities. Their native outputs differ, so HAKI uses tool-specific parsers that normalize severities, messages, locations, and identifiers. Normalization makes cross-tool presentation possible while preserving tool-specific evidence for investigation.', 'Finding severity is represented by INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, and CRITICAL. A robust normalized record should retain a stable fingerprint, tool/rule identifier, package or file location, line range, concise message, and metadata required to reproduce the result. Severity mapping must be documented because tools use different taxonomies and a normalized label is an interpretation.'),
('Isolation, reliability, and limits', 'Scanner output is evidence, not a guarantee. A clean result means the selected tools and rules found no reportable item under their versions and configuration. It does not prove absence of defects. False positives, unsupported languages, stale vulnerability databases, and scanner execution failure must be visible. Resource controls should limit CPU, memory, disk, runtime, concurrency, and network access.', 'The code creates and cleans a workspace, but production isolation also depends on Docker configuration. Workers should run with restricted capabilities, no privileged mode, no host mounts, no Docker socket, and bounded network egress. Queue retries need class-specific handling and idempotent persistence. Record scanner versions, image digests, rule commits, timing, and exit statuses for reproducibility.'),
]),
('Testing and Quality Assurance', [
('Test strategy', 'Testing should progress from pure unit functions to database and HTTP integration tests, then to end-to-end browser flows. Parsers can be tested with fixture reports. Authentication services need token lifecycle and password tests. Middleware requires origin, rate-limit, and role tests. Queue processors need success, scanner failure, archive failure, retry, and cleanup cases. UI tests should cover navigation, responsive layout, and local storage behavior.', 'The server package defines a Vitest test command and the repository contains test suites for authentication, token refresh, rate limiting, CSRF/origin checks, GitHub App authentication, and scanner finding parsers. The supplied report previously claimed a specific test count; this report avoids freezing that number unless the suite is run at report-generation time. Test status should be recorded with the actual command output.'),
('Security and integration verification', 'Security testing should verify that no unauthenticated caller can trigger or read another user’s validation run, team separation is maintained, suspended installations are rejected, malformed IDs do not expose internal errors, and refresh-token reuse invalidates the family. Archive tests should include path traversal, symlinks, oversized compressed data, corrupt archives, and excessive file counts.', 'GitHub integration tests can use generated key pairs and mocked HTTP responses for JWT, installation tokens, repository archive retrieval, rate limits, and permission errors. Scanner tests should use safe fixtures that contain representative findings but no real secrets. End-to-end testing with a real GitHub test app should be isolated to a dedicated test organization and explicitly avoid modifying production repositories.'),
('Quality metrics and evidence', 'Useful quality indicators include test coverage by critical workflow, defect escape rate, validation job success/failure ratio, queue age, scanner duration, stale approval detection, and mean recovery time. Metrics should guide improvement rather than be presented as proof of security. Every reported result needs a date, environment, command, and scope so a reviewer can reproduce it.', 'For this report, implementation evidence comes from the inspected source tree, Prisma schema, route registration, worker code, and test files. A fresh build and test run should be included in the final academic version after the student verifies local service dependencies. No benchmark claims are made because no controlled performance study was provided. Screenshots document actual user interface appearance, not backend correctness.'),
]),
('Implementation and Deployment', [
('Monorepo and local development', 'The repository is a JavaScript package workspace with `client`, `server`, `cli`, and shared packages. The frontend starts through Vite and builds into `client/dist`. The server is developed with TypeScript and `tsx`, compiled with `tsc`, and tested with Vitest. Prisma schema and migrations define the relational model. Docker Compose under `infrastructure/docker` describes supporting services.', 'A local developer should install dependencies from the workspace lockfile, copy and populate environment examples, start PostgreSQL and Redis, apply database migrations, then launch the API and client. The worker is started separately for validation tasks. A root-level setup guide should list exact prerequisites and ports; this report does not include or reveal actual credentials.'),
('Implementation status by layer', 'The client implements a public landing page, authentication modal, authenticated dashboard, repository create/import experiences, repository workspace, file browsing, settings, About page, theme controls, and chatbot component. The browser repository store is local. The backend has an Express route composition, health endpoint, auth/OAuth modules, access token and refresh rotation, database schema, submissions validation endpoints, review routes, and asynchronous validation code.', 'Not all modules form one end-to-end user journey yet. The current client local storage model is not the same as the server’s PostgreSQL model. CLI is scaffolding. Repository listing and import from real GitHub, policy administration, complete review orchestration, pull-request publication, notifications, and production deployment need further verification. This status inventory is important for an honest demonstration.'),
('Deployment and release controls', 'A deployment process should build immutable frontend and backend artifacts, apply migrations in a controlled step, deploy workers with pinned scanner images, and validate health before shifting traffic. Database backups and restore drills should be routine. Secrets should be injected from a managed store. Logs must redact sensitive values. A release should include rollback criteria and a migration compatibility plan.', 'The health route reports API health but does not prove that PostgreSQL, Redis, GitHub, or scanners are ready. A production readiness endpoint should check required dependencies with bounded timeouts. CI should run lint, unit/integration tests, type checks, frontend build, migration validation, dependency scanning, and container scanning. No production deployment is claimed in this report.'),
]),
('User Experience and Interface', [
('Navigation and hierarchy', 'The HAKI interface is organized around the user’s workspace. The public landing page describes the product and provides entry points for sign-in. The authenticated shell exposes repository, settings, import, and About areas. The dashboard emphasizes a welcome heading, activity visualization, repository summary, recent activity, and a new repository action. A consistent logo and brand palette help users recognize the product across public and workspace screens.', 'The current design uses a teal accent, neutral surfaces, rounded controls, and both dark and light appearance states. The interface has been refined through repeated review of the hero heading, chatbot color, navigation, repository file view, and logo placement. In reports, screenshots should be accompanied by numbered captions that describe the visible user task, not only the picture itself.'),
('Repository file browsing', 'The repository workspace displays a hierarchical tree and a selected file view. Folder nodes expand to reveal children; choosing a file updates the selected path and source panel. README content is rendered below the source view in the repository root experience to resemble familiar code-hosting interfaces. The selected file can be opened on a dedicated code route for a more focused view, with navigation back to the repository.', 'This interaction is implemented in `RepositoryWorkspace.jsx` and `DirectoryTree.jsx`. File and repository data are retrieved from the client service layer. The experience should communicate loading and error states quickly, preserve the selected path in the URL, support keyboard navigation, and distinguish folders from files using both icon and accessible label. The screenshots in this report show the public page only; detailed file-view screenshots should be captured during final project demonstration when available.'),
('Accessibility and usability evaluation', 'Usability depends on readable typography, adequate contrast, labels for icon-only buttons, focus indicators, predictable route behavior, and responsive layouts. The account modal should label email and password fields and expose clear error text. Theme toggles should provide accessible names. The file tree should expose expansion state and selected file state. Motion should respect reduced-motion preferences and should not delay access to core actions.', 'The project uses semantic page and control elements in many screens and includes labels and aria attributes in important controls. A formal accessibility evaluation should use keyboard-only walkthroughs, browser accessibility tooling, contrast checks, and screen-reader tests. Usability should be evaluated with task completion and observed confusion, especially for sign in, new repository, import, file navigation, and returning from a dedicated code page.'),
]),
('Results, Limitations and Future Scope', [
('Results achieved', 'The project results include a coherent monorepo structure, a React workspace UI, a relational data model for identity, teams, repositories, submissions, reviews, validation, pull requests, findings, and audit events, and an Express API foundation. Authentication uses Argon2id and signed access tokens, with refresh token rotation and reuse handling. Validation jobs are separated into a BullMQ worker pipeline and results are normalized before persistence.', 'The interface supports a local walkthrough of the public HAKI page, dashboard, repository organization, file tree, code display, README placement, settings, import, and About screens. The project offers a practical base for future governance integration. These outcomes are source-backed; they do not by themselves demonstrate production readiness or a fully integrated cloud deployment.'),
('Limitations', 'The client stores local demonstration data in browser local storage and therefore does not provide shared durable repository state. The CLI is empty scaffolding. Backend models and routes include more workflow concepts than the currently connected UI. GitHub App credentials and external service configuration are environment-dependent. Complete team administration, live repository synchronization, commit-bound approval enforcement, controlled publishing, notifications, and audit coverage require integration and verification.', 'Security scanners also have limits. Findings can be false positive or incomplete, scanner data changes over time, and an empty result does not prove safety. Worker hardening and operational controls must be reviewed in actual deployment manifests. No load test, production penetration test, or usability study was supplied, so this report makes no numerical performance or user satisfaction claim.'),
('Roadmap and conclusion', 'A staged roadmap is recommended: first unify client and server persistence; then complete team and repository authorization; implement submission creation and commit updates; make validation status and findings visible in the UI; add review and SHA-bound approval rules; integrate pull-request publishing behind a fail-closed gate; implement CLI; add notifications and audit reporting; and complete security, accessibility, and load testing.', 'HAKI’s central contribution is a clear design for connecting repository changes to validation evidence and accountable review without replacing Git. The codebase already contains meaningful foundations in authentication, persistence, scanner processing, and user-facing repository workflows. The next phase is integration: make the user-visible workflow rely on the same source of truth as the backend, then verify its security and operational behavior against realistic repositories.'),
]),
('Conclusion and References', [
('Conclusion', 'HAKI addresses a real coordination problem in collaborative development: source history, automated checks, human decisions, and publishing controls need to refer to the same revision. The project’s schema represents repositories, submissions, validation runs, findings, reviews, pull requests, and audit events. Its API separates request handling from asynchronous scanning. Its client gives users an accessible workspace for repositories and file contents.', 'The most important technical principle in the design is commit-bound evidence. A policy result or approval should not float independently from the revision that was evaluated. The second principle is clear implementation honesty: a UI prototype, a backend foundation, and a production deployment are different maturity levels. HAKI should move toward a unified end-to-end flow through tested increments.'),
('Learning outcomes', 'The project exercises full-stack development, monorepo organization, API contract validation, relational data modeling, authentication, background processing, integration design, scanner output parsing, and UI composition. It also highlights how security requirements influence workflow and architecture. Rate limits, refresh-token reuse detection, workspace cleanup, and role checks are examples of controls that must be considered alongside ordinary feature development.', 'Future academic evaluation can ask whether validation and review refer to the same commit, what happens when a worker crashes, how a suspended GitHub installation is handled, and whether a user can access another team’s findings. Answering these questions requires source inspection and tests, not only a demonstration screenshot. This report’s appendices provide a practical checklist for that evaluation.'),
('References and attribution', 'Technical descriptions in this report are grounded in the HAKI source tree and in primary documentation for Git, GitHub Apps, OWASP security guidance, Docker resource controls, Node.js, Express, PostgreSQL, Prisma, Redis, BullMQ, Gitleaks, Semgrep, and Trivy. Product-specific implementation claims should be confirmed against the source at the time the report is submitted, since the repository can evolve after this snapshot.', 'Bibliographic links are listed in Appendix H. Referenced web documentation is accessed on 26 November 2026. The example academic PDF is used only as a structural reference for front matter and chapter organization; its project name, student identities, text, and figures have not been reused as HAKI project facts.'),
]),
('Appendices', [
('Appendix orientation', 'The appendices provide implementation-oriented reference material that supports the main chapters. They include a glossary, requirement traceability, selected data dictionary entries, route inventory, test scenarios, setup and walkthrough guidance, and a risk register. These records are intended to make the report useful for review and future development rather than merely adding length.', 'Status descriptions reflect source inspection at the time this report was prepared. They should be revisited whenever a feature is wired into the client, a migration changes the database, or an integration becomes available. No secrets, tokens, or private repository source are included in these examples.'),
('Demonstration evidence', 'Screenshots and diagrams serve different roles. A screenshot records the visible interface at a point in time. A diagram explains structure or flow. Screenshots should be taken from the running HAKI application and should avoid exposing credentials, private repository names, or personal browser data. Each figure should have a number, caption, and reference in the surrounding discussion.', 'The report includes a capture of the running HAKI public landing page and source-grounded diagrams for architecture, validation flow, entity relationships, and state transitions. UI behavior beyond the captured screen is described from the project implementation and identified as prototype behavior where relevant.'),
('Maintenance of this report', 'When the project matures, replace provisional design language with verified outcomes. Add the test command and actual results, record scanner versions and configuration fingerprints, capture current authenticated screens, and update the implementation matrix. Remove obsolete screenshots and update page references after a major edit. Keep the report and source version under version control so that the report can be associated with a specific code revision.', 'The report should be reviewed before academic submission for the student’s enrollment number, supervisor details, institute wording, project session, and required signatures. The title page uses the available institute and supervisor information from the supplied HAKI report, while the student enrollment field remains blank because no verified enrollment number was provided.'),
]),
]

def draw_diagram(path, title, boxes, arrows=None, width=1500, height=820):
    im=Image.new('RGB',(width,height),'white'); d=ImageDraw.Draw(im)
    try: font=ImageFont.truetype('/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial.ttf',28)
    except: font=ImageFont.load_default()
    try: tf=ImageFont.truetype('/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial.ttf',36)
    except: tf=font
    d.text((40,24),title,fill='#172033',font=tf)
    coords=[]
    n=len(boxes); cols=3 if n>4 else 2; rows=(n+cols-1)//cols
    left,top=70,120; gapx=80; gapy=65; bw=(width-left*2-gapx*(cols-1))//cols; bh=(height-top-80-gapy*(rows-1))//rows
    for i,label in enumerate(boxes):
        row,col=divmod(i,cols); x=left+col*(bw+gapx); y=top+row*(bh+gapy)
        coords.append((x,y,x+bw,y+bh))
        d.rounded_rectangle(coords[-1],radius=24,fill='#EAF3F2',outline='#438C89',width=4)
        lines=textwrap.wrap(label,28)
        text='\n'.join(lines); box=d.multiline_textbbox((0,0),text,font=font,spacing=10)
        d.multiline_text((x+(bw-(box[2]-box[0]))/2,y+(bh-(box[3]-box[1]))/2),text,fill='#172033',font=font,align='center',spacing=10)
    if arrows:
        for a,b,label in arrows:
            ax1,ay1,ax2,ay2=coords[a]; bx1,by1,bx2,by2=coords[b]
            x1=(ax1+ax2)//2; y1=(ay1+ay2)//2; x2=(bx1+bx2)//2; y2=(by1+by2)//2
            import math
            dx,dy=x2-x1,y2-y1
            sx=min((ax2-ax1)/2/max(abs(dx),1),(ay2-ay1)/2/max(abs(dy),1))
            tx=min((bx2-bx1)/2/max(abs(dx),1),(by2-by1)/2/max(abs(dy),1))
            x1=int(x1+dx*sx); y1=int(y1+dy*sx); x2=int(x2-dx*tx); y2=int(y2-dy*tx)
            d.line((x1,y1,x2,y2),fill='#667085',width=4)
            ang=math.atan2(y2-y1,x2-x1); L=18
            p1=(x2-L*math.cos(ang-.5),y2-L*math.sin(ang-.5));p2=(x2-L*math.cos(ang+.5),y2-L*math.sin(ang+.5))
            d.polygon([(x2,y2),p1,p2],fill='#667085')
            if label: d.text(((x1+x2)//2,(y1+y2)//2-28),label,fill='#344054',font=font)
    im.save(path)

